@@ -0,0 +1,168 @@
+# SPEC: Stock reservations
+
+## Why
+
+Checkout has to hold stock for a cart while payment is in progress. That
+way the units can't be sold to someone else before the payment settles.
+After payment, the hold is either confirmed into a normal order or released.
+Carts get abandoned and payments fail silently, so a hold nobody confirms
+must lapse by itself. Nothing in the package runs a background job, so the
+lapse can't depend on someone calling a sweep. The cart service retries
+requests, so a repeated reserve must not create a second hold. The payment
+service retries confirm, so a repeated confirm must not create a second
+order.
+
+## Interface (fixed; hidden tests call exactly this)
+
+`InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` keeps working as is.
+
+- `InventoryService.reserve(warehouse: str, items: dict[str, int], idempotency_key: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.confirm_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Order`
+- `InventoryService.release_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.get_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.available(sku, warehouse)` must take holds into account.
+- `inventory.models`: dataclass `Reservation` with `reservation_id: str`,
+  `warehouse: str`, `items: dict[str, int]` (units held per SKU),
+  `status: ReservationStatus`, `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`;
+  enum `ReservationStatus` with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`,
+  `EXPIRED`.
+- `inventory.errors`: new `ReservationNotFound`, `ReservationExpired`,
+  `InvalidReservationState`, `IdempotencyConflict` (all subclass
+  `InventoryError`); reuse the existing errors where they fit.
+- Time comes only from the injected clock. There is no background job:
+  expiry must take effect without anyone calling a sweep.
+
+The constructor may also accept an optional hold duration (TTL). It
+defaults to **20 minutes**. Existing three-argument calls must behave exactly
+as before.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Requested items**: the `items` argument passed to `reserve`.
+- **Held items**: `Reservation.items`, the units actually held per SKU. These
+  can be fewer than requested (see partial holds).
+- **Held (per SKU and warehouse)**: the sum of held units over all
+  reservations that are `ACTIVE` and not yet expired.
+- **Expired**: a reservation that is not confirmed or released, at any clock
+  time `now >= expires_at`.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Availability
+
+1. `available(sku, wh) == max(0, on_hand - allocated - held)`.
+2. `allocated(sku, wh)` still counts only `PLACED` orders. Holds never appear
+   in it.
+3. `on_hand` is unaffected by reserve, release and expiry. None of them
+   writes to the ledger.
+4. Units held by a reservation can't be taken by `place_order` or by another
+   `reserve` while the hold is active.
+5. A released or expired hold stops reducing `available` from that moment.
+   For expiry, that moment is the instant the clock reaches `expires_at`, and
+   no other call is needed for it to take effect.
+
+### reserve
+
+6. A new reservation has status `ACTIVE`, `order_id is None`, and
+   `expires_at == clock.now() + TTL` (20 minutes by default).
+7. **Partial holds**: each requested line holds
+   `min(requested, available right now)` units.
+8. A line with nothing available is left out of `Reservation.items`. It does
+   not appear with 0.
+9. If no line can hold anything, `reserve` raises `InsufficientStock` and
+   creates nothing: no reservation, and the key is not recorded.
+10. If every line is fully available, `items` equals the requested items.
+11. Validation matches `place_order`. An unknown warehouse raises
+    `UnknownWarehouse`, an unknown SKU raises `UnknownSku`, and a quantity
+    that isn't a positive int (including `bool`) raises `InvalidQuantity`.
+12. Empty `items` and an empty `idempotency_key` are both rejected with some
+    `InventoryError`. The spec doesn't say which subclass.
+13. A rejected request (criteria 9, 11, 12) holds nothing, not even for its
+    valid lines. It leaves no trace, so its key can be used again later.
+14. Each reservation gets a unique `reservation_id`. The id format isn't
+    specified.
+
+### Idempotency
+
+15. Keys are global across all warehouses and are remembered forever,
+    whatever later happens to the reservation.
+16. If `reserve` is called with a key that was already used successfully,
+    and the same warehouse and the same requested items (dict equality, so
+    key order doesn't matter), it returns the original reservation in its
+    current state. That state can be `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED` or
+    `EXPIRED`. The call creates no new reservation, holds nothing more and
+    does not change `expires_at`.
+17. The comparison is against the **requested** items, not the held items.
+    A replay of a request that was only partly held still counts as the same
+    request.
+18. If the key was used with a different warehouse or different requested
+    items, `reserve` raises `IdempotencyConflict`. This applies even if the
+    original reservation is confirmed, released or expired.
+
+### get_reservation
+
+19. Returns the reservation and reports its current status. An `ACTIVE` hold
+    that has passed `expires_at` is reported as `EXPIRED`, without anyone
+    having called a sweep.
+20. Expired, released and confirmed reservations stay retrievable.
+21. An unknown id raises `ReservationNotFound`. The same applies to
+    `confirm_reservation` and `release_reservation`.
+
+### confirm_reservation
+
+22. On an active, unexpired reservation, confirm creates a `PLACED` order in
+    the same warehouse. The order has one line per held SKU with exactly the
+    held quantity, so a partial hold yields an order for the held units only.
+    `placed_at` is the current clock time.
+23. After confirm, the reservation is `CONFIRMED` and its `order_id` is the
+    new order's id. The units move from held to allocated with no gap:
+    `available` is the same immediately before and after confirm.
+24. The resulting order behaves like any other order. `ship_order` and
+    `cancel_order` work on it.
+25. Confirming an already `CONFIRMED` reservation returns the same order
+    (same `order_id`) and creates nothing. This holds at any time, including
+    after `expires_at`, and after the order has been shipped or cancelled.
+26. Confirming an expired reservation (at or after `expires_at`) raises
+    `ReservationExpired` and creates no order.
+27. Confirming a `RELEASED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`.
+28. If on-hand stock has dropped below what is held (for example through
+    `adjust`), confirm may either succeed or raise `InsufficientStock`.
+    Whichever happens, it must not leave a half-done state: either there is an
+    order and the reservation is `CONFIRMED`, or there is no order and the
+    reservation is unchanged.
+
+### release_reservation
+
+29. Releasing an active, unexpired reservation sets it to `RELEASED` and
+    frees its units immediately.
+30. Releasing an already `RELEASED` reservation returns it unchanged without
+    raising.
+31. Releasing an expired reservation is a no-op. It returns the reservation
+    with status `EXPIRED` without raising.
+32. Releasing a `CONFIRMED` reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`, and
+    the order is unaffected.
+
+### Compatibility
+
+33. All existing tests pass unchanged. Orders, ledger, reports and the
+    three-argument constructor behave as before when no reservations exist.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Extending or refreshing a hold.
+- Background jobs, timers or a required sweep call. Expiry is purely a
+  function of the clock.
+- Concurrency or multi-process safety. The package is in-memory and
+  single-threaded.
+- Reservations spanning several warehouses.
+- Reviving a reservation when its order is cancelled. It stays `CONFIRMED`.
+- Persisting reservations beyond the in-memory repository.
+
+## Left to the implementer
+
+- Id format for reservations.
+- Which `InventoryError` subclass is used for empty `items` and an empty
+  `idempotency_key`.
+- Whether to add a public `held(sku, warehouse)` query or a held column in
+  `stock_report`.
+- Outcome of criterion 28 (succeed or raise `InsufficientStock`).
